## 1. Objective and scope

Develop and execute a Bayesian empirical study to:

- Quantify the magnitude, persistence, and uncertainty of tensile-strength differences among nominally identical 3D-printed polymer coupons.
- Partition variation among material, printer, build, location, coupon, conditioning, and testing sources.
- Estimate which measurable manufacturing and testing factors account for the differences.
- Distinguish competing explanations, including:
  - true coupon-to-coupon microstructural differences;
  - build- or printer-level process variation;
  - raw-material and moisture effects;
  - geometry and density variation;
  - environmental conditioning;
  - tensile-machine, grip, alignment, and operator effects;
  - apparent differences caused by sampling or measurement error.
- Assess whether controlling identified factors reduces residual variation to a prespecified practically acceptable level.

Primary outcome: ultimate tensile strength (UTS), calculated using measured coupon dimensions. Secondary outcomes: elastic modulus, yield strength where applicable, strain at break, fracture location, and failure mode.

The scope should initially be restricted to one polymer formulation, coupon geometry, nominal print profile, and build orientation. Any generalization to other materials, geometries, or print settings requires a subsequent confirmatory phase.

## 2. Assumptions

- “Nominally identical” means coupons share the same digital geometry, nominal material, slicing profile, orientation, and target conditioning and test protocol.
- Coupons remain physically distinguishable through unique, blinded identifiers and complete chain-of-custody records.
- The polymer lot is sufficiently homogeneous to support within-lot inference, while lot-to-lot variation is separately estimable if multiple lots are included.
- Tensile specimens and procedures follow one prespecified standard appropriate to the polymer and manufacturing method; departures are documented.
- Observations within a common print build, machine, spool, operator session, or test batch may be correlated and therefore cannot be treated as independent.
- Strength may depend nonlinearly on process conditions and may exhibit heteroscedasticity or occasional outliers.
- Nondestructive measurements can precede tensile testing without materially changing coupon properties. If this is uncertain, validate it on designated specimens.
- Missingness, rejected prints, and premature or grip-region failures may be informative and will not be silently excluded.
- Prior distributions will be set before outcome analysis, justified from engineering constraints, pilot metrology, historical quality-control information, or weakly informative regularization.
- A practically meaningful strength difference, denoted \(\Delta_{\text{practical}}\), and an acceptable residual coefficient of variation will be specified with engineering stakeholders before data collection.

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: Measurement-system qualification

Before studying print variability:

1. Calibrate dimensional, mass, environmental, thermal, load, displacement, and strain instruments using traceable references where available.
2. Conduct a crossed gauge repeatability and reproducibility study for:
   - width and thickness;
   - mass;
   - extensometer placement and strain measurement;
   - fixture alignment and gripping;
   - image-based or volumetric measurements.
3. Use reference specimens or a stable surrogate to quantify tensile-frame repeatability without consuming the principal experimental coupons.
4. Compare UTS based on nominal area versus measured local cross-sectional area; designate the latter as primary if measurement reliability is adequate.
5. Prespecify rules for fixture slippage, grip breaks, machine faults, and invalid strain readings.

Proceed only if measurement error is small enough to resolve \(\Delta_{\text{practical}}\), or revise the methods.

### Phase B: Baseline replication under nominal conditions

Print replicated coupons across multiple independent builds, days, printers, spool segments, and build-plate positions while holding nominal settings constant.

Use a balanced, partially crossed design where feasible:

- At least two printers of the same model, or repeated printer states if only one printer is available.
- Multiple independent builds per printer and day.
- Coupons distributed across predefined plate regions and orientations.
- Multiple material spools or independently sampled spool segments, preferably nested within one lot and replicated across lots if lot inference is desired.
- Random assignment of spool, printer, build order, and plate layout subject to operational constraints.
- Randomized and blinded testing order, blocked across tensile-test sessions and operators.
- Coupons from each build split across test sessions to avoid confounding build with test day.
- Coupons from different builds interleaved within each test session.

This phase estimates repeatability, reproducibility, clustering, time drift, and whether persistent rankings occur among printers, builds, or plate positions.

### Phase C: Randomized mechanism-discrimination experiment

Select manipulable factors supported by baseline data and physical plausibility. Use a randomized factorial or Bayesian optimal design, including center points and deliberately repeated nominal conditions. Candidate factors include:

- filament moisture or drying protocol;
- actual nozzle temperature;
- bed or chamber temperature;
- extrusion multiplier/material flow;
- print speed and cooling;
- layer height or raster conditions;
- spool age or storage exposure;
- printer maintenance/nozzle wear state;
- conditioning temperature, humidity, and duration;
- test speed, alignment intervention, or grip protocol.

Avoid changing too many factors relative to available replication. Prioritize factors using baseline posterior uncertainty, engineering plausibility, and expected information gain. Include selected interactions, particularly moisture × temperature, temperature × speed, and geometry/density × processing conditions.

To discriminate process causes from test causes:

- Split coupons from each print build across test machines, operators, sessions, and randomized test order where possible.
- Test coupons from different builds within the same test block.
- Rotate or re-fixture a validation subset where the standard permits.
- Include stable reference specimens in each test session to detect test-system drift.
- Blind test personnel to manufacturing condition and hypothesized group.

To discriminate persistent latent subgroup effects:

- Produce longitudinal repeat builds under identical nominal settings.
- Sample material from early, middle, and late spool positions.
- Map build-plate positions and print sequence.
- Record machine state before and after maintenance events.
- If nondestructive imaging is available, measure void fraction, cross-sectional geometry, and defect localization before destructive testing.

### Phase D: Confirmatory replication

Freeze the model structure and candidate explanation from Phases A–C. Conduct an independently randomized replication using new builds and, if relevant, a new material lot or printer. Include:

- the original nominal condition;
- the proposed controlled or corrected condition;
- conditions designed to separate the leading explanation from its strongest competitor.

### Sample-size strategy

Determine sample sizes by Bayesian assurance simulation before collection, not by a fixed conventional power formula. Simulate from plausible prior predictive distributions under competing mechanisms and choose replication sufficient to achieve, with high probability:

- narrow posterior intervals for key variance components;
- high posterior probability of detecting effects at least as large as \(\Delta_{\text{practical}}\);
- reliable discrimination among leading causal models;
- acceptable precision for predicted strength under nominal and corrected conditions.

The effective unit for printer-, build-, spool-, or session-level effects is the number of independently replicated units at that level, not the number of coupons.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

For every coupon, retain linked records for:

- unique coupon, build, printer, spool, material lot, operator, test session, and machine identifiers;
- source file, slicer version, firmware, toolpath checksum, and complete nominal settings;
- build date, start time, order, plate coordinates, in-plane orientation, and coupon print sequence;
- printer age, nozzle identity and wear, calibration, maintenance history, and interruptions;
- filament diameter measurements, spool mass history, storage duration, drying history, and moisture measurement;
- logged nozzle, bed, chamber, and ambient temperature and humidity;
- actual extrusion or feed behavior, print duration, power interruptions, alarms, and failed-print records;
- conditioning environment, duration, and elapsed time between printing, conditioning, measurement, and testing;
- local width and thickness at prespecified gauge positions, mass, density or apparent density, surface roughness, and warpage;
- nondestructive measurements such as void fraction, bead geometry, dimensional maps, or thermal-history proxies where feasible;
- tensile-frame identity, calibration status, load cell, grips, alignment checks, preload, test speed, operator, and test order;
- raw force, displacement, extensometer strain, and synchronized metadata rather than only derived outcomes;
- UTS, modulus, yield measure, strain at break, fracture position, fracture angle, and categorical failure mode;
- photographs of specimens before and after testing;
- protocol deviations, missing observations, print rejections, grip failures, censored outcomes, and reasons for exclusion.

Evidence relevant to competing explanations includes:

- Persistence within a printer/build/spool over time: supports stable manufacturing-source effects.
- Spatial plate gradients reproducible across builds: supports thermal or position effects.
- Association with measured moisture and reversal after randomized drying: supports moisture causation.
- Association with void fraction, density, local area, or fusion morphology: supports microstructural mediation.
- Disappearance after correcting local cross-sectional area: supports geometric measurement artifacts.
- Strength shifts with test machine, operator, session, order, grip behavior, or alignment: supports testing-system effects.
- Similar shifts in stable reference specimens: supports test drift rather than print variation.
- Differences that survive cross-testing and are predicted by pretest coupon measurements: support genuine specimen differences.
- Apparent group effects that shrink substantially in the hierarchical model: support sampling variability or pseudoreplication as the explanation.
- Residual multimodality after measured factors are included: supports an unmeasured process state or mixture of failure mechanisms.

## 5. Measurement

### Tensile response

- Apply one prespecified tensile standard consistently.
- Measure width and thickness at multiple gauge locations using a validated method.
- Define primary stress using the prespecified measured cross-sectional area. Report sensitivity analyses using nominal and minimum local area.
- Derive outcomes from raw curves with locked algorithms and visually audit a blinded sample.
- Record whether failure occurs in the gauge section, near a transition, or in the grips.
- Treat grip failures and interrupted tests according to prespecified validity and censoring rules.

### Manufacturing factors

Distinguish settings from actual process measurements. Logged temperature, humidity, extrusion behavior, and timings are preferred to nominal values.

For moisture:

- use a validated direct or indirect method;
- measure immediately before printing when possible;
- record sampling position and elapsed exposure time.

For internal structure:

- quantify apparent density or void fraction;
- define image-analysis thresholds before association with strength is examined;
- evaluate repeatability and orientation sensitivity.

### Blinding and quality control

- Hide manufacturing-condition labels from dimensional and tensile-test operators.
- Randomize coupon presentation and test order.
- Use duplicate measurements and reference artifacts at prespecified intervals.
- Store raw data in immutable form with audit trails.
- Resolve discrepancies using rules established before unblinding.

### Measurement models

Represent observed quantities as noisy measurements of latent true values. For example:

\[
w^{obs}_{ir}=w_i+\epsilon^{(w)}_{ir}, \qquad
t^{obs}_{ir}=t_i+\epsilon^{(t)}_{ir}
\]

and propagate dimensional uncertainty into area and stress rather than treating measured dimensions as error-free. Apply analogous models to moisture, temperature, density, and other error-prone predictors when their uncertainty is non-negligible.

## 6. Analysis

### Primary hierarchical model

Model coupon strength on a positive scale, using a Student-\(t\) likelihood or a lognormal model if posterior predictive checks support it. A representative formulation is:

\[
\log(y_i) =
\alpha
+ X_i\beta
+ f(Z_i)
+ u_{\text{lot}[i]}
+ u_{\text{spool}[i]}
+ u_{\text{printer}[i]}
+ u_{\text{build}[i]}
+ u_{\text{position}[i]}
+ u_{\text{test machine}[i]}
+ u_{\text{session}[i]}
+ u_{\text{operator}[i]}
+ \epsilon_i ,
\]

where:

- \(X_i\) contains prespecified manufacturing, specimen, conditioning, and testing predictors;
- \(f(Z_i)\) contains justified nonlinear terms and limited interactions;
- random effects follow the actual nested or crossed design;
- residual scale may depend on process condition if heteroscedasticity is plausible.

If repeated tensile outcomes are not possible for the same coupon, use repeated metrology and crossed allocation to separate measurement and testing variability from between-coupon variability.

### Priors

- Use a weakly informative prior for the grand mean based on physically plausible polymer strength.
- Use regularizing zero-centered priors for standardized fixed effects.
- Use half-normal or half-Student-\(t\) priors for variance components.
- Use stronger shrinkage for exploratory interactions and nonlinear terms.
- Predeclare skeptical priors for effects lacking physical support.
- Conduct prior predictive checks to exclude implausible strengths, variance, and factor effects.

### Variance decomposition

Estimate posterior distributions for:

- lot, spool, printer, build, position, test-machine, session, operator, and residual variance;
- intraclass correlations;
- measurement-error contribution;
- total coefficient of variation under nominal conditions.

Report uncertainty in each component rather than a single point estimate.

### Explanatory contribution

Evaluate how measured factors account for persistent differences by comparing posterior predictive variance:

1. before adding measured covariates;
2. after adding manufacturing covariates;
3. after adding geometry and structure variables;
4. after adding testing variables.

Quantify attenuation of printer, build, or subgroup effects after covariate inclusion. Treat attenuation as descriptive unless the design supplies causal identification.

For hypothesized mediation, fit a joint model in which randomized process factors affect moisture, geometry, density, or void fraction, which in turn predict strength. Report direct and mediated effects with clear causal assumptions.

### Competing explanations

Create explicit generative models for:

- pure sampling and measurement variation;
- stable printer or build offsets;
- continuous measured-factor effects;
- latent subgroup or process-state mixtures;
- testing-system drift;
- microstructure-mediated effects.

Compare them using posterior predictive checks and out-of-sample predictive performance, preferably leave-one-build- or leave-one-session-out validation. Bayes factors may be used only with carefully justified proper priors and accompanied by sensitivity analysis.

Key discriminating predictions include:

- A test-system explanation predicts that reference specimens and coupons from multiple builds shift together by test session.
- A print-process explanation predicts clustering by build or printer despite randomized test allocation.
- A moisture explanation predicts an exposure-response relationship and a change under randomized drying.
- A geometry explanation predicts that the difference diminishes when accurate local area is used.
- A microstructure explanation predicts that pretest density or imaging features forecast strength and mediate randomized process effects.
- A latent-state explanation predicts reproducible multimodal process or failure signatures, not merely heavy-tailed residuals.

### Model checking and robustness

- Conduct prior and posterior predictive checks.
- Examine residuals by build, position, printer, test session, operator, and time.
- Check calibration and predictive coverage for held-out builds.
- Test sensitivity to priors, likelihood choice, exclusion rules, dimension definitions, outliers, and missing-data assumptions.
- Model invalid or missing tests jointly where exclusion probability may depend on strength or condition.
- Avoid dichotomizing conclusions by whether a credible interval crosses zero. Report posterior effect distributions, practical-equivalence probabilities, and predicted outcomes.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Specify thresholds before outcome analysis.

A factor will be considered an important contributor when:

1. the randomized design or defensible identification assumptions support causal interpretation;
2. the posterior probability that its effect exceeds \(\Delta_{\text{practical}}\) is at least 0.95;
3. its direction and magnitude replicate in the confirmatory phase;
4. including it materially reduces relevant unexplained variance or improves held-out-build prediction;
5. posterior predictive checks show no major model failure.

A factor may be considered practically negligible when the posterior probability that its effect lies within a prespecified region of practical equivalence is at least 0.95.

A proposed explanation will be favored over a competitor when it makes a prespecified distinguishing prediction that is observed in randomized or held-out data and has clearly better predictive performance. If models remain indistinguishable, report both rather than selecting one.

Sequential enrollment may be reviewed only at planned checkpoints. Continue collecting independent builds rather than additional within-build coupons when uncertainty is dominated by build-level variance. Stop when all of the following hold:

- posterior interval widths for principal effects and variance components meet prespecified precision targets;
- the leading explanation satisfies the evidential criteria above or all practically relevant candidate effects are ruled into the equivalence region;
- held-out predictive calibration is acceptable;
- confirmatory replication agrees with the discovery phase.

Also stop for futility if predictive simulations show that the maximum feasible additional sample is unlikely to resolve the leading explanations. Stop or redesign if measurement-system error exceeds the target resolution.

## 8. Limitations

- Destructive tensile testing prevents repeated strength measurements on the same coupon, making separation of coupon and test error dependent on crossed allocation, reference specimens, and validated metrology.
- Results may not generalize beyond the studied polymer, lot range, geometry, orientation, slicer, printer model, or environmental conditions.
- Some variables—such as local interlayer bonding or transient thermal history—may remain imperfectly measured.
- Sensors may not capture conditions at the precise location and time relevant to bond formation.
- Observational associations from the baseline phase can remain confounded; causal claims should rely primarily on randomized interventions.
- Process factors may interact, so one-factor explanations may be incomplete.
- Maintenance, spool changes, and instrument drift may be partly aliased with calendar time unless deliberately crossed.
- Mixture and nonlinear models can be weakly identified with limited independent builds and may be sensitive to priors.
- Imaging or other nondestructive measurements may alter handling, conditioning time, or specimen temperature.
- Excluding malformed coupons or grip failures can bias results; including them requires careful modeling of validity and censoring.
- A residual “coupon effect” does not by itself identify a physical mechanism.
- Bayesian conclusions depend on model structure and priors; prior predictive validation, sensitivity analysis, independent replication, and transparent reporting are therefore essential.

