A factor may be considered practically negligible when the posterior probability that its effect lies within a prespecified region of practical equivalence is at least 0.95.

A proposed explanation will be favored over a competitor when it makes a prespecified distinguishing prediction that is observed in randomized or held-out data and has clearly better predictive performance. If models remain indistinguishable, report both rather than selecting one.

Sequential enrollment may be reviewed only at planned checkpoints. Continue collecting independent builds rather than additional within-build coupons when uncertainty is dominated by build-level variance. Stop when all of the following hold:

- posterior interval widths for principal effects and variance components meet prespecified precision targets;
- the leading explanation satisfies the evidential criteria above or all practically relevant candidate effects are ruled into the equivalence region;
- held-out predictive calibration is acceptable;
- confirmatory replication agrees with the discovery phase.

Also stop for futility if predictive simulations show that the maximum feasible additional sample is unlikely to resolve the leading explanations. Stop or redesign if measurement-system error exceeds the target resolution.

## 8. Limitations

- Destructive tensile testing prevents repeated strength measurements on the same coupon, making separation of coupon and test error dependent on crossed allocation, reference specimens, and validated metrology.
- Results may not generalize beyond the studied polymer, lot range, geometry, orientation, slicer, printer model, or environmental conditions.
- Some variables—such as local interlayer bonding or transient thermal history—may remain imperfectly measured.
- Sensors may not capture conditions at the precise location and time relevant to bond formation.
- Observational associations from the baseline phase can remain confounded; causal claims should rely primarily on randomized interventions.
- Process factors may interact, so one-factor explanations may be incomplete.
- Maintenance, spool changes, and instrument drift may be partly aliased with calendar time unless deliberately crossed.
- Mixture and nonlinear models can be weakly identified with limited independent builds and may be sensitive to priors.
- Imaging or other nondestructive measurements may alter handling, conditioning time, or specimen temperature.
- Excluding malformed coupons or grip failures can bias results; including them requires careful modeling of validity and censoring.
- A residual “coupon effect” does not by itself identify a physical mechanism.
- Bayesian conclusions depend on model structure and priors; prior predictive validation, sensitivity analysis, independent replication, and transparent reporting are therefore essential.
